the supply, at the same bar
  the same walk over the same positions, keeping the stack's own type names
  instead of the host's. The list is hand-written and is printed below, so a
  name missing from it undercounts this side and makes the obligation look
  further from met than it is.

  arvo               571
  gone               966
  notko              223

  230 host, 966 naming an arvo crate or type that is not on
  arvo's `dev`, and 794 resolving. So 11% of the positions carrying
  any of the three still name the host, and a further 48%
  name arvo and reach nothing. Counting the second group as supply, which
  one undivided arvo list does, is what makes the obligation look nearly
  met when half of what meets it does not exist.

